1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,667
2 PM. Full sun on your rooftop solar…

2
00:00:03,707 --> 00:00:05,725
and suddenly, a power cut

3
00:00:06,278 --> 00:00:07,697
The sun is still shining

4
00:00:07,737 --> 00:00:08,954
But your fans stop

5
00:00:08,994 --> 00:00:10,910
Your solar isn't broken

6
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:12,889
It switched off on purpose

7
00:00:12,929 --> 00:00:14,534
— to protect a lineman's life

8
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:17,098
Where does all that sunshine go?

9
00:00:17,138 --> 00:00:20,000
And how do you keep your fans running? Let's see

10
00:00:21,763 --> 00:00:24,462
One thing first — this is not the inverter

11
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:25,949
battery you keep at home…

12
00:00:25,989 --> 00:00:28,692
It's the box your solar panels connect to

13
00:00:28,732 --> 00:00:31,663
Installers call it an on-grid inverter…

14
00:00:31,703 --> 00:00:33,787
We'll just call it the solar box

15
00:00:34,306 --> 00:00:36,084
Your panels give DC power

16
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:37,594
— it flows only one way

17
00:00:38,119 --> 00:00:40,062
Your house runs on AC power

18
00:00:40,102 --> 00:00:41,816
— not the air conditioner,

19
00:00:41,856 --> 00:00:43,232
the wall-socket kind

20
00:00:43,272 --> 00:00:44,573
It swings forward and back

21
00:00:44,613 --> 00:00:46,597
— 50 full swings every second

22
00:00:46,637 --> 00:00:48,421
The solar box turns panel

23
00:00:48,461 --> 00:00:50,490
power into wall-socket power

24
00:00:52,124 --> 00:00:52,825
Here's the thing

25
00:00:52,865 --> 00:00:54,529
— the solar box can't make that

26
00:00:54,569 --> 00:00:56,646
back-and-forth rhythm on its own

27
00:00:56,686 --> 00:00:58,672
Think of the grid as a drummer…

28
00:00:58,712 --> 00:01:00,462
and the solar box as a dancer

29
00:01:00,502 --> 00:01:02,517
that only follows the drum

30
00:01:02,968 --> 00:01:05,295
On a normal day, it follows every beat

31
00:01:05,335 --> 00:01:07,505
Your house uses what it needs

32
00:01:07,545 --> 00:01:09,646
The extra goes out through your meter…

33
00:01:09,686 --> 00:01:11,044
onto the street wire…

34
00:01:11,084 --> 00:01:13,071
and up to the transformer on the pole

35
00:01:14,653 --> 00:01:16,223
Now the power cut comes

36
00:01:16,263 --> 00:01:17,856
The electricity board switches

37
00:01:17,896 --> 00:01:19,185
off the supply to your street

38
00:01:19,717 --> 00:01:22,652
Think about it. If your solar box kept pushing,

39
00:01:22,692 --> 00:01:25,023
that dead wire would be alive again, right?

40
00:01:25,498 --> 00:01:27,230
And that transformer on

41
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:29,233
the pole works in reverse

42
00:01:29,273 --> 00:01:30,301
Your 230 volts…

43
00:01:30,341 --> 00:01:32,535
becomes about 11,000 volts…

44
00:01:32,575 --> 00:01:36,245
On a wire the lineman thinks is switched off

45
00:01:37,858 --> 00:01:40,221
So the solar box is built to notice

46
00:01:40,261 --> 00:01:41,713
If the beat goes missing,

47
00:01:41,754 --> 00:01:43,450
or the voltage starts changing,

48
00:01:43,490 --> 00:01:45,610
it switches itself off… click

49
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,184
Safety rules say it must

50
00:01:47,224 --> 00:01:49,355
switch off within 2 seconds

51
00:01:50,811 --> 00:01:52,694
But there's one tricky case

52
00:01:52,734 --> 00:01:54,598
Say your whole street is using

53
00:01:54,638 --> 00:01:56,864
exactly what the solar panels make

54
00:01:56,904 --> 00:01:59,021
Then the power cut changes nothing

55
00:01:59,061 --> 00:02:00,351
— the box can't feel it

56
00:02:00,813 --> 00:02:02,674
So the box tests the drummer

57
00:02:02,714 --> 00:02:05,672
Again and again, it plays a little off-beat

58
00:02:05,712 --> 00:02:07,406
— on purpose — to check…

59
00:02:07,446 --> 00:02:08,851
is the drummer still there?

60
00:02:09,927 --> 00:02:11,335
If the grid is there,

61
00:02:11,375 --> 00:02:13,525
the drummer pulls it back in line

62
00:02:14,499 --> 00:02:16,977
If the grid is gone, nobody pulls it back

63
00:02:17,017 --> 00:02:18,696
The beat keeps sliding…

64
00:02:18,736 --> 00:02:20,401
and the box knows. Click

65
00:02:20,441 --> 00:02:21,392
— it switches off

66
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:24,465
What happens to your panels?

67
00:02:24,505 --> 00:02:25,806
Once the box is off,

68
00:02:25,846 --> 00:02:27,698
the power has nowhere to go

69
00:02:27,738 --> 00:02:28,689
Nothing comes out

70
00:02:29,186 --> 00:02:30,523
The sunlight still lands

71
00:02:30,563 --> 00:02:31,995
and just turns into heat

72
00:02:32,035 --> 00:02:34,001
— your panels are built for that

73
00:02:34,041 --> 00:02:35,435
But the panel wires still

74
00:02:35,475 --> 00:02:36,439
have voltage in them

75
00:02:36,478 --> 00:02:38,255
So don't touch them yourself

76
00:02:38,294 --> 00:02:39,929
— leave that to a trained person

77
00:02:41,443 --> 00:02:43,684
So how do you keep your fans running?

78
00:02:43,724 --> 00:02:44,981
You need a different box

79
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:46,347
— a hybrid inverter

80
00:02:46,387 --> 00:02:48,381
Hybrid simply means it works with

81
00:02:48,421 --> 00:02:50,156
the grid and with a battery

82
00:02:50,558 --> 00:02:52,802
When the grid dies, a switch inside it

83
00:02:52,842 --> 00:02:55,155
first cuts your house off from the street

84
00:02:55,195 --> 00:02:57,625
Then the battery becomes the drummer,

85
00:02:57,665 --> 00:02:59,172
and the panels dance again

86
00:02:59,793 --> 00:03:00,629
Remember this

87
00:03:00,669 --> 00:03:03,857
A normal on-grid solar box, with no battery,

88
00:03:03,897 --> 00:03:06,171
has to go silent when the grid goes

89
00:03:06,211 --> 00:03:07,243
That's by design

90
00:03:08,501 --> 00:03:10,893
If anyone offers to switch off this safety

91
00:03:10,933 --> 00:03:11,879
— just say no…

92
00:03:11,919 --> 00:03:13,791
Because the lineman fixing your

93
00:03:13,831 --> 00:03:16,229
street is the one who gets the shock

94
00:03:17,346 --> 00:03:18,802
Your solar never failed

95
00:03:18,842 --> 00:03:20,230
It just lost its drummer

96
00:03:20,799 --> 00:03:23,429
But when the grid is live, how does that box

97
00:03:23,469 --> 00:03:26,097
turn sunlight into steady wall-socket power?

98
00:03:26,137 --> 00:03:27,435
Let's open it up next
